# Every text file checks out LF, on every platform. The suite reads the checkout
# as text — a component's markup, a stylesheet rule, a script's own source — and
# pins lines of it, so a Windows clone with core.autocrlf=true, which wrote those
# files CRLF, failed a test the macOS and Linux legs pass. Fixing that in each
# test would enumerate the readers; this fixes the bytes they all read. The index
# was already LF (autocrlf normalizes on commit), so no file's content changes,
# only what a checkout writes. A clone made before this line keeps its CRLF files
# until they are rewritten; on a clean tree:
#   git rm --cached -r -q . && git reset --hard
* text=auto eol=lf

# Shell scripts must be LF even on Windows: git-bash runs them, and CRLF turns
# `#!/usr/bin/env bash` into an unfindable interpreter and every command
# substitution into a value with a trailing carriage return. The v1.6.0 Windows
# release job failed on exactly that class of bug.
*.sh text eol=lf
# Same reasoning for anything else a POSIX shell or a WASM runtime reads verbatim.
*.mjs text eol=lf
# These two predate the rule above and stay: `text` is stricter than `auto`, which
# leaves alone a file that sniffs as binary (docs/head-to-head/score-round.mjs
# does) and so would not normalize one committed CRLF.

# A CSV's line terminator is data, not formatting. Six eval fixtures were
# committed CRLF, and `git add --renormalize .` under the rule above rewrites
# every line of them to LF. A line-ending fix should not quietly change the
# evidence an eval ran against, so these keep the bytes they were authored with.
*.csv -text
# `auto` sniffs for binary content, and a PDF can be plain ASCII. Taken for text,
# a commit would turn its CRLFs into LFs and move every byte its xref table
# points at.
*.pdf binary
